// heartsim tutorial: first steps with the simulator. Original text by the heartsim authors,
// MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-app/src/tutorial.rs.
Tutorial(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "01_basics",
    title: "Basics: from one heartbeat to the ECG",
    summary: "Follow a single beat through the conduction system, freeze it on the strip, read the heart rate and meet your first abnormal rhythm.",
    references: [
        "Goldberger AL, Goldberger ZD, Shvilkin A. Goldberger's Clinical Electrocardiography: A Simplified Approach, 9th ed. Elsevier 2017 (ch. 1-2).",
        "Kligfield P et al. Recommendations for the standardization and interpretation of the electrocardiogram, part I. Circulation 2007;115:1306-1324.",
        "Burns E, Buttner R. Life in the Fast Lane ECG Library: ECG basics. litfl.com/ecg-library.",
    ],
    pages: [
        Page(
            title: "Welcome",
            markdown: r#"
Welcome to **heartsim**. Every page of a tutorial sets the simulator up for you: the rhythm, the
speed and the view change as you turn the pages, so you can read and watch at the same time.

On the left of the Simulator tab is the **3-D heart**. Its colours show the electrical state of the
tissue: resting muscle is drawn in cool colours, depolarised (excited) muscle in warm colours.

At the bottom is the **ECG strip**. It is computed from the same simulation, so every deflection
on the strip belongs to something you can see happening in the heart.

Use **Next** to continue. You can leave the tutorial at any time; your place is remembered.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(CameraPreset(Ap)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
                Ui(Resume),
            ],
        ),
        Page(
            title: "One beat in slow motion",
            markdown: r#"
The simulation now runs at one fifth of real time (see the highlighted **speed** slider).

Watch one beat travel through the heart:

1. The **sinus node**, high in the right atrium, fires on its own.
2. The wave spreads over both **atria**: this produces the **P wave**.
3. The **AV node** holds the impulse back for a moment. Nothing large is depolarising, so the strip
   stays flat: this pause is most of the **PR interval**.
4. The **His bundle**, the **bundle branches** and the **Purkinje fibres** carry the impulse quickly
   to the inner surface of both ventricles.
5. The **ventricles** depolarise from the inside out: the large, fast **QRS complex**.
6. The ventricles recover: the broad **T wave**.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(0.2)),
                Ui(Highlight("speed")),
            ],
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Freeze a moment",
            markdown: r#"
The simulation has just been **paused** a few milliseconds after the start of a QRS complex.

While paused you can scroll back along the strip with the **s back** slider. The 3-D heart follows
the cursor: whatever instant the strip shows, the heart shows the same instant. Move back to the P
wave and compare the colour of the atria with that of the ventricles.

Press **Run** (the highlighted button) when you want the simulation to continue.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
                Ui(SetSpeed(0.5)),
                Ui(WaitForEvent(Qrs)),
                Ui(WaitForEvent(ElapsedMs(40.0))),
                Ui(Pause),
                Ui(Highlight("pause")),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which event does the QRS complex record?",
                choices: [
                    "Depolarisation of the atria",
                    "Depolarisation of the ventricles",
                    "Recovery (repolarisation) of the ventricles",
                    "Conduction through the AV node",
                ],
                answer: 1,
                explanation: "The QRS is the depolarisation of the ventricular muscle. Conduction through the AV node is too small to be seen on the surface ECG; ventricular recovery is the T wave.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Reading the heart rate",
            markdown: r#"
The sinus node has just been set to a faster rate.

On standard ECG paper running at **25 mm/s** one small square is 0.04 s and one large square
(five small ones) is 0.2 s. Three hundred large squares pass in one minute, so a quick estimate is:

> heart rate ≈ 300 ÷ (number of large squares between two R waves)

One large square between beats is 300/min, two is 150, three is 100, four is 75, five is 60.
For irregular rhythms count the complexes in a 10-second strip and multiply by six.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(Resume),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
                Sim(SetGlobalSinusRate(bpm: 100.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "At 25 mm/s the R waves are three large squares apart. What is the heart rate?",
                choices: ["60 /min", "100 /min", "150 /min", "75 /min"],
                answer: 1,
                explanation: "300 ÷ 3 = 100 beats per minute.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "A first abnormal rhythm",
            markdown: r#"
The rhythm list (highlighted) now shows **first-degree AV block**.

Every P wave is still followed by a QRS complex, but the delay between them is longer than normal.
The normal **PR interval** is 120–200 ms (three to five small squares); here it is longer.
In the heart you can see the impulse wait longer in the AV node before the ventricles light up.

Try the calipers under the strip to measure the PR interval yourself.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("avb_1st")),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(Highlight("rhythm_picker")),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which measurement is abnormal in first-degree AV block?",
                choices: ["The QRS duration", "The QT interval", "The PR interval", "The heart rate"],
                answer: 2,
                explanation: "First-degree AV block is a PR interval above 200 ms with every P wave conducted.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Where to go next",
            markdown: r#"
You have followed a beat through the conduction system, frozen it on the strip, estimated the
heart rate and recognised a prolonged PR interval.

Good next steps:

* the **Cardiac axis** tutorial, which explains how the limb leads tell you the direction of
  ventricular depolarisation;
* the **Rhythm list** above the strip, which holds every rhythm in the library;
* the **Rhythm Quiz** tab, to test yourself.

Press **Finish** to close the tutorial.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("sinus_normal")),
            ],
        ),
    ],
)
